WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.250
Film Courage: Can any book be made into a
movie?

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.100
Dr. Ken Atchity, Author/Producer: Any book?

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.100
Film Courage: Any book.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.250
Dr. Atchity: No.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:07.970
I don’t think so.

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:11.799
Like an instruction manual?

00:00:11.799 --> 00:00:14.370
People have made instruction manuals into
movies?

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:15.940
But no, not any book.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:22.240
But what makes the movie is absolute drama.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:23.500
You have to have drama.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:28.230
And I’m not talking about a bad movie, I’m
talking about a good movie that people will

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:37.590
not fast forward through or switch channels
on or to make a worthy movie a book has to

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:45.240
have drama and that means it has to have a
very clear three acts (beginning, middle and

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:53.030
end) and what defines the beginning is something
that hooks the audience into the story so

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:58.430
that it will not abandon the story and an
ending is something that makes you leave the

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:05.790
theater or turn off your television very satisfied
with the way the story ended whether it’s

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:12.050
happy or sad isn’t the point but satisfying
like the end of WITNESS where we realize that

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:16.780
the lovers cannot stay together even though
we want them to because it just wouldn’t

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.300
make any sense.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:25.540
And the way the last shot is Peter Weir taking
a long time to let the car drive down the

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:27.960
lane away from the farm onto the road.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.780
It’s such a long shot that it gives you
a chance to go through your head and think

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:39.120
“Oh c’mon, slow down, turn around, make
a U-turn or run after him.”

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.830
And none of that happens because your mind
is now going “That wouldn’t make any sense.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:44.830
This is sad.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:47.480
It’s got to end but it still was beautiful.”

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:51.350
But that’s good drama and good movie making.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:55.230
But it’s the hardest part for any writer.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:02:00.920
It has to be something that keeps you there
meaning filled with twists and turns and reversals

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:06.830
and unexpected events and so on that you don’t
want to tune out.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:12.880
And if a book can do that, then it can be
a movie.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:18.091
And if a book isn’t doing that but has potential
because it has a strong protagonist and a

00:02:18.091 --> 00:02:22.450
strong antagonist that’s where a treatment
comes in.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:27.430
You write a treatment and fix all the problems
with the book in the treatment and pitch the

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:28.430
treatment.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:33.330
I’ve sold a number of movies based on a
treatment because the book had problems and

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:37.680
I almost wouldn’t let the buyers read the
book because once they said they want to do

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.950
it I go “Okay let’s just go with the treatment
please!”

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:46.330
But at the end they are ready to sign, they
have to look at the book and then they’ll

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:49.150
say “I see what you mean.”

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:51.290
But the treatment solves the problems.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:53.490
You know if the middle is not dramatic enough.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:58.960
Most especially the problem with novels is
the ending is not powerful enough and clear

00:02:58.960 --> 00:02:59.960
enough.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:06.489
The third act is not clear enough where you
see that there’s a turning point that goes

00:03:06.489 --> 00:03:08.840
fro act two to act three.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:16.710
Because editors in New York are not as demanding
as audiences are in a movie theater.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:23.250
They don’t really look for turning points
the same way an audience does.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.601
There are exceptions to all of this stuff.

00:03:25.601 --> 00:03:31.780
None of it is rules but basically a three-act
structure needs to be there and has to be

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:32.780
dramatic.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.500
All three acts have to be dramatic.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.480
You have to have a protagonist that you can
relate to.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.590
You don’t have to like her but you have
to relate to her.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:46.250
And you have to have an antagonist who is
worth of her and is in every way as strong

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:50.340
as she is because otherwise the ending is
predictable.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:55.620
And the stronger the antagonist, the stronger
the protagonist and the stronger the story

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:56.780
is.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:01.370
So I hope that answers the question.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:05.910
Books that have at least some of those elements
can be turned into movies.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:12.129
I actually used to have a class that’s called
‘Designing Your Novel To Be A Film.’

00:04:12.129 --> 00:04:16.100
Because the best place for that is on your
original drawing board.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:22.589
Make sure that when you design your novel
that you include these things in your novel

00:04:22.589 --> 00:04:26.519
and that way you won’t be disappointed when
nobody wants to make it into a film.

